pendell@hotmail.com wrote:
> The bard songs will be a combination of ring, amulet, scroll, or book
> effects.  Some of the effects will be quite positive (remove curse,
> scare monster for N turns) others will be negative (destroy armor,
> amnesia).

> What tunes do what is randomized at the beginning of every game. Every
> bard effect is assigned to a random 5-note tune.  Two low-level songs
> will be given at game start. Others can be revealed through prayer or
> through discovering them written on scrolls or on the walls of the
> dungeon.

Would you discover what the effect is at the same time, or would the only
way be to try and see?  If the latter, this would be about equivalent to
blindly reading scrolls or quaffing potions -- which is generally
considered a good way to get you killed.

Experienced players generally only use a magical item that they haven't
identified (or at least verified that it can't be harmful) as a last
option in an emergency.

> But 90% of bard effects are uncovered through simple experimentation --
> the bard starts plunking away at his instrument to see what tunes do
> what different things.  For instance, if he plays AAAAA and his helmet
> turns to dust and blows away, he knows not to play that again.

There are 16807 possible tunes.  Just trying all of them to find the
useful ones would make pudding farming look interesting.  For this to
work, you would need some way of discovering or working out the correct
tune (similar in function to the mastermind game at the castle.)

There's another danger if you added something of that nature, though:
the standard way to start playing a bard would be to lock yourself in
a closet on dlvl 1 and work out what all of the tunes are.  Not
a very good game mechanic.

On a slightly different note, what would the interface be like?  Do
you have to write down/remember all of the tunes, and then type them
in when you want to use them?  That worked OK in the Zelda games,
but with the tunes changing every time you play, it probably wouldn't
work here.  I assume that you would probably end up having a menu 
similar to casting spells.

> Playing a musical tune costs Pw, regardless of success, failure, or
> effect.

This doesn't make much sense to me.  What about playing mastermind
at the castle, which doesn't involve any magic -- why should that
cost magical power?  And why should it cost power to play a scale?

If there was some way to select whether you're trying to play a
magical tune or not, that might work, though adding that as an
extra prompt would be an annoying interface.

It would make a lot more sense to me to have a specific kind of
instrument used to play magic tunes -- a magic flute, an enchanted
hurdy-gurdy, whatever.  I see what you posted about being able
to find another instrument and use it, but if you start with an
instrument, that situation will almost never come up anyway.


Besides the comments on the specifics you mentioned, there's
one basic problem that I see: when you get down to it, this
is really just an alternative to the current spellcasting
system.  You learn spells (tunes) from items found in the dungeon,
and then cast (play) them using your magic power.  There is the
requirement that you have an instrument, but if you start with
it then it makes very little difference.  You would have
to come up with a method of differentiating it from 
spellcasting before it would be worth implementing.

